<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Trekboers</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Trekboers were semi-nomadic, Dutch-speaking livestock farmers who moved beyond the settled districts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-colony">Cape Colony</a> in the 18th century. They took over grazing land from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khoekhoe">Khoikhoi</a> and San communities and pushed the frontier east, where they came into conflict with the Xhosa along the Fish River. They are among the ancestors of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afrikaners">Afrikaners</a> and the later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/voortrekkers">Voortrekkers</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/trekboers.jpg" alt="Trekboers" width="320" height="231" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Samuel Daniell · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Daniell_Trekboer.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Trekboers began moving inland from the districts around what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-town">Cape Town</a>, such as Stellenbosch, founded in 1679, and Paarl and Franschhoek, settled from 1688, in the late 17th century and throughout the 18th. In part they grew out of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a>'s loan farm system, under which the company leased grazing rights over large but less fertile farms, which encouraged farmers to move with their flocks in search of seasonal pasture. Others sought to escape the company's rule, which they believed corrupt and indifferent to the free <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burghers">burghers</a>, the class to which most of them belonged.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>From 1700 the Trekboers crossed the Berg River into the Tulbagh basin; in the early 1710s they spread into the Olifants River valley and the Bokkeveld, by the 1740s into the Little Karoo and by the 1760s into the Great Karoo. Between 1699 and 1725, when the company lifted its ban on livestock trading, their trade with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khoekhoe">Khoikhoi</a> largely meant stripping them of their herds. Many Khoikhoi, having lost their livestock, became servants on Dutch-speaking farms. The San lost water, game and grazing, and San and some Khoi resisted with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> attacks that halted the advance after about 1770.</p><p>The company had tried to stop settlers moving beyond the western Cape, but the authorities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-town">Cape Town</a> lacked the means to police the frontier. In 1795 groups of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boers</a> rebelled against company rule and set up republics at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/graaff-reinet">Graaff-Reinet</a> and, on 17 June, at Swellendam. The British, who took Cape Town in September 1795, tightened government oversight, and tensions came to a head in the Slachter's Nek Rebellion of 1815, quickly put down with its leaders executed. Dissatisfaction with British rule, constant wars with the Xhosa and shortage of land eventually led many to join the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-trek">Great Trek</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Some had a loan farm as a base, sometimes shared by several families, but the poorer Trekboers lived in their wagons and seldom stayed long in one place. Their hardy herds of local stock linked the interior with the suppliers of ships at the Cape. Their identity rested on Christian faith, customs of European origin and militia practices. They spoke a variety of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> they called die taal, the language, from which Eastern Border Afrikaans developed. Many continued as nomadic pastoralists well into the 19th century.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Trekboers” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The San resisted by attacking the Boers and raiding their stock, and the <b>Trekboers</b> answered with commandos.</blockquote><a href="/culture/san-people">San people · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Trekboer</b> cattle farmers pushed the frontier east to the Fish River, where they met the Xhosa, and Afrikaans developed from Cape Dutch.</blockquote><a href="/state/dutch-cape-colony">Dutch Cape Colony</a></li></ul></section>
